result.append("\011Bermuda\0111944\0111945\011-\011Mar\011Sun>=8\011 2:00\0111:00\011D\012Rule\011Bermuda\0111944\0111945\011-\011Nov\011Sun>=1\011 2:00\0110\011S\012Rule\011Bermuda\0111947\011only\011-\011May\011Sun>=15\011 2:00\0111:00\011D\012Rule\011Bermuda\0111947\011only\011-\011Sep\011Sun>=8\011 2:00\0110\011S\012Rule\011Bermuda\0111948\0111952\011-\011May\011Sun>=22\011 2:00\0111:00\011D\012Rule\011Bermuda\0111948\0111952\011-\011Sep\011Sun>=1\011 2:00\0110\011S\012Rule\011Bermuda\0111956\011only\011-\011May\011Sun>=22\011 2:00\0111:00\011D\012Rule\011Bermuda\0111956\011only\011-\011Oct\011lastSun\011 2:00\0110\011S\012\012# Zone\011NAME\011\011STDOFF\011RULES\011FORMAT\011[UNTIL]\012\011\011#STDOFF\011-4:19:18.3\012Zone Atlantic/Bermuda\011-4:19:18 -\011LMT\0111890\011# Hamilton\012\011\011\011-4:19:18 Bermuda BMT/BST 1930 Jan 1  2:00\012\011\011\011-4:00\011Bermuda\011A%sT\0111974 Apr 28  2:00\012\011\011\011-4:00\011Canada\011A%sT\0111976\012\011\011\011-4:00\011US\011A%sT\012\012# Costa Rica\012\012# Milne gives -5:36:13.3 as San Jos\303\251 mean time.\012\012# Rule\011NAME\011FROM\011TO\011-\011IN\011ON\011AT\011SAVE\011LETTER/S\012Rule\011CR\0111979\0111980\011-\011Feb\011lastSun\0110:00\0111:00\011D\012Rule\011CR\0111979\0111980\011-\011Jun\011Sun>=1\0110:00\0110\011S\012Rule\011CR\0111991\0111992\011-\011Jan\011Sat>=15\0110:00\0111:00\011D\012# IATA SSIM (1991-09) says the following was at 1:00;\012# go with Shanks & Pottenger.\012Rule\011CR\0111991\011only\011-\011Jul\011 1\0110:00\0110\011S\012Rule\011CR\0111992\011only\011-\011Mar\01115\0110:00\0110\011S\012# There are too many San Jos\303\251s elsewhere, so we'll use 'Costa Rica'.\012# Zone\011NAME\011\011STDOFF\011RULES\011FORMAT\011[UNTIL]\012\011\011#STDOFF\011-5:36:13.3\012Zone America/Costa_Rica\011-5:36:13 -\011LMT\0111890        # San Jos\303\251\012\011\011\011-5:36:13 -\011SJMT\0111921 Jan 15 # San Jos\303\251 Mean Time\012\011\011\011-6:00\011CR\011C%sT\012# Coco\012# no information; probably like America/Costa_Rica\012\012# Cuba\012\012# From Paul Eggert (2013-02-21):\012# Milne gives -5:28:50.45 for the observatory at Havana, -5:29:23.57\012# for the port, and -5:30 for meteorological observations.\012# For now, stick with Shanks & Pottenger.\012\012# From Arthur David Olson (1999-03-29):\012# The 1999-03-28 exhibition baseball game held in Havana, Cuba, between\012# the Cuban National Team and the Baltimore Orioles was carried live on\012# the Orioles Radio Network, including affiliate WTOP in Washington, DC.\012# During the game, play-by-play announcer Jim Hunter noted that\012# \042We'll be losing two hours of sleep...Cuba switched to Daylight Saving\012# Time today.\042  (The \042two hour\042 remark referred to losing one hour of\012# sleep on 1999-03-28 - when the announcers were in Cuba as it switched\012# to DST - and one more hour on 1999-04-04 - when the announcers will have\012# returned to Baltimore, which switches on that date.)\012\012# From Steffen Thorsen (2013-11-11):\012# DST start in Cuba in 2004 ... does not follow the same rules as the\012# years before.  The correct date should be Sunday 2004-03-28 00:00 ...\012# https://web.archive.org/web/20040402060750/http://www.granma.cu/espanol/2004/marzo/sab27/reloj.html\012\012# From Evert van der Veer via Steffen Thorsen (2004-10-28):\012# Cuba is not going back to standard time this year.\012# From Paul Eggert (2006-03-22):\012# http://www.granma.cu/ingles/2004/septiembre/juev30/41medid-i.html\012# says that it's due to a problem at the Antonio Guiteras\012# thermoelectric plant, and says \042This October there will be no return\012# to normal hours (after daylight saving time)\042.\012# For now, let's assume that it's a temporary measure.\012\012# From Carlos A. Carnero Delgado (2005-11-12):\012# This year (just like in 2004-2005) there's no change in time zone\012# adjustment in Cuba.  We will stay in daylight saving time:\012# http://www.granma.cu/espanol/2005/noviembre/mier9/horario.html\012\012# From Jesper N\303\270rgaard Welen (2006-10-21):\012# An article in GRANMA INTERNACIONAL claims that Cuba will end\012# the 3 years of permanent DST next weekend, see\012# http://www.granma.cu/ingles/2006/octubre/lun16/43horario.html\012# \042On Saturday night, October 28 going into Sunday, October 29, at 01:00,\012# watches should be set back one hour - going back to 00:00 hours - returning\012# to the normal schedule....\012\012# From Paul Eggert (2007-03-02):\012# <http://www.granma.cubaweb.cu/english/news/art89.html>, dated yesterday,\012# says Cuban clocks will advance at midnight on March 10.\012# For lack of better information, assume Cuba will use US rules,\012# except that it switches at midnight standard time as usual.\012#\012# From Steffen Thorsen (2007-10-25):\012# Carlos Alberto Fonseca Arauz informed me that Cuba will end DST one wee");
result.append("k\012# earlier - on the last Sunday of October, just like in 2006.\012#\012# He supplied these references:\012#\012# http://www.prensalatina.com.mx/article.asp?ID={4CC32C1B-A9F7-42FB-8A07-8631AFC923AF}&language=ES\012# http://actualidad.terra.es/sociedad/articulo/cuba_llama_ahorrar_energia_cambio_1957044.htm\012#\012# From Alex Krivenyshev (2007-10-25):\012# Here is also article from Granma (Cuba):\012#\012# Regir\303\241 el Horario Normal desde el pr\303\263ximo domingo 28 de octubre\012# http://www.granma.cubaweb.cu/2007/10/24/nacional/artic07.html\012#\012# http://www.worldtimezone.com/dst_news/dst_news_cuba03.html\012\012# From Arthur David Olson (2008-03-09):\012# I'm in Maryland which is now observing United States Eastern Daylight\012# Time. At 9:44 local time I used RealPlayer to listen to\012# http://media.enet.cu/radioreloj\012# a Cuban information station, and heard\012# the time announced as \042ocho cuarenta y cuatro\042 (\042eight forty-four\042),\012# indicating that Cuba is still on standard time.\012\012# From Steffen Thorsen (2008-03-12):\012# It seems that Cuba will start DST on Sunday, 2007-03-16...\012# It was announced yesterday, according to this source (in Spanish):\012# http://www.nnc.cubaweb.cu/marzo-2008/cien-1-11-3-08.htm\012#\012# Some more background information is posted here:\012# https://www.timeanddate.com/news/time/cuba-starts-dst-march-16.html\012#\012# The article also says that Cuba has been observing DST since 1963,\012# while Shanks (and tzdata) has 1965 as the first date (except in the\012# 1940's). Many other web pages in Cuba also claim that it has been\012# observed since 1963, but with the exception of 1970 - an exception\012# which is not present in tzdata/Shanks. So there is a chance we need to\012# change some historic records as well.\012#\012# One example:\012# http://www.radiohc.cu/espanol/noticias/mar07/11mar/hor.htm\012\012# From Jesper N\303\270rgaard Welen (2008-03-13):\012# The Cuban time change has just been confirmed on the most authoritative\012# web site, the Granma.  Please check out\012# http://www.granma.cubaweb.cu/2008/03/13/nacional/artic10.html\012#\012# Basically as expected after Steffen Thorsen's information, the change\012# will take place midnight between Saturday and Sunday.\012\012# From Arthur David Olson (2008-03-12):\012# Assume Sun>=15 (third Sunday) going forward.\012\012# From Alexander Krivenyshev (2009-03-04)\012# According to the Radio Reloj - Cuba will start Daylight Saving Time on\012# midnight between Saturday, March 07, 2009 and Sunday, March 08, 2009-\012# not on midnight March 14 / March 15 as previously thought.\012#\012# http://www.worldtimezone.com/dst_news/dst_news_cuba05.html\012# (in Spanish)\012\012# From Arthur David Olson (2009-03-09)\012# I listened over the Internet to\012# http://media.enet.cu/readioreloj\012# this morning; when it was 10:05 a. m. here in Bethesda, Maryland the\012# the time was announced as \042diez cinco\042 - the same time as here, indicating\012# that has indeed switched to DST. Assume second Sunday from 2009 forward.\012\012# From Steffen Thorsen (2011-03-08):\012# Granma announced that Cuba is going to start DST on 2011-03-20 00:00:00\012# this year. Nothing about the end date known so far (if that has\012# changed at all).\012#\012# Source:\012# http://granma.co.cu/2011/03/08/nacional/artic01.html\012#\012# Our info:\012# https://www.timeanddate.com/news/time/cuba-starts-dst-2011.html\012#\012# From Steffen Thorsen (2011-10-30)\012# Cuba will end DST two weeks later this year. Instead of going back\012# tonight, it has been delayed to 2011-11-13 at 01:00.\012#\012# One source (Spanish)\012# http://www.radioangulo.cu/noticias/cuba/17105-cuba-restablecera-el-horario-del-meridiano-de-greenwich.html\012#\012# Our page:\012# https://www.timeanddate.com/news/time/cuba-time-changes-2011.html\012#\012# From Steffen Thorsen (2012-03-01)\012# According to Radio Reloj, Cuba will start DST on Midnight between March\012# 31 and April 1.\012#\012# Radio Reloj has the following info (Spanish):\012# http://www.radioreloj.cu/index.php/noticias-radio-reloj/71-miscelaneas/7529-cuba-aplicara-el-horario-de-verano-desde-el-1-de-abril\012#\012# Our info on it:\012# https://www.timeanddate.com/news/time/cuba-starts-dst-2012.html\012\012# From Steffen Thor");
