result.append("# tzdb data for South America and environs\012\012# This file is in the public domain, so clarified as of\012# 2009-05-17 by Arthur David Olson.\012\012# This file is by no means authoritative; if you think you know better,\012# go ahead and edit the file, and please send any changes to\012# the public mailing list tz@iana.org for general use in the future.\012# For more, please see the file CONTRIBUTING in the tz distribution.\012\012# From Paul Eggert (2016-12-05):\012#\012# Unless otherwise specified, the source for data through 1990 is:\012# Thomas G. Shanks and Rique Pottenger, The International Atlas (6th edition),\012# San Diego: ACS Publications, Inc. (2003).\012# Unfortunately this book contains many errors and cites no sources.\012#\012# Many years ago Gwillim Law wrote that a good source\012# for time zone data was the International Air Transport\012# Association's Standard Schedules Information Manual (IATA SSIM),\012# published semiannually.  Law sent in several helpful summaries\012# of the IATA's data after 1990.  Except where otherwise noted,\012# IATA SSIM is the source for entries after 1990.\012#\012# For data circa 1899, a common source is:\012# Milne J. Civil time. Geogr J. 1899 Feb;13(2):173-94.\012# https://www.jstor.org/stable/1774359\012#\012# These tables use numeric abbreviations like -03 and -0330 for\012# integer hour and minute UT offsets.  Although earlier editions used\012# alphabetic time zone abbreviations, these abbreviations were\012# invented and did not reflect common practice.\012\012###############################################################################\012\012###############################################################################\012\012# Argentina\012\012# From Bob Devine (1988-01-28):\012# Argentina: first Sunday in October to first Sunday in April since 1976.\012# Double Summer time from 1969 to 1974.  Switches at midnight.\012\012# From U. S. Naval Observatory (1988-01-19):\012# ARGENTINA           3 H BEHIND   UTC\012\012# From Hernan G. Otero (1995-06-26):\012# I am sending modifications to the Argentine time zone table...\012# AR was chosen because they are the ISO letters that represent Argentina.\012\012# Rule\011NAME\011FROM\011TO\011-\011IN\011ON\011AT\011SAVE\011LETTER/S\012Rule\011Arg\0111930\011only\011-\011Dec\011 1\0110:00\0111:00\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111931\011only\011-\011Apr\011 1\0110:00\0110\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111931\011only\011-\011Oct\01115\0110:00\0111:00\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111932\0111940\011-\011Mar\011 1\0110:00\0110\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111932\0111939\011-\011Nov\011 1\0110:00\0111:00\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111940\011only\011-\011Jul\011 1\0110:00\0111:00\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111941\011only\011-\011Jun\01115\0110:00\0110\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111941\011only\011-\011Oct\01115\0110:00\0111:00\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111943\011only\011-\011Aug\011 1\0110:00\0110\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111943\011only\011-\011Oct\01115\0110:00\0111:00\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111946\011only\011-\011Mar\011 1\0110:00\0110\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111946\011only\011-\011Oct\011 1\0110:00\0111:00\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111963\011only\011-\011Oct\011 1\0110:00\0110\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111963\011only\011-\011Dec\01115\0110:00\0111:00\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111964\0111966\011-\011Mar\011 1\0110:00\0110\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111964\0111966\011-\011Oct\01115\0110:00\0111:00\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111967\011only\011-\011Apr\011 2\0110:00\0110\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111967\0111968\011-\011Oct\011Sun>=1\0110:00\0111:00\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111968\0111969\011-\011Apr\011Sun>=1\0110:00\0110\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111974\011only\011-\011Jan\01123\0110:00\0111:00\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111974\011only\011-\011May\011 1\0110:00\0110\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111988\011only\011-\011Dec\011 1\0110:00\0111:00\011-\012#\012# From Hernan G. Otero (1995-06-26):\012# These corrections were contributed by InterSoft Argentina S.A.,\012# obtaining the data from the:\012# Talleres de Hidrograf\303\255a Naval Argentina\012# (Argentine Naval Hydrography Institute)\012Rule\011Arg\0111989\0111993\011-\011Mar\011Sun>=1\0110:00\0110\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0111989\0111992\011-\011Oct\011Sun>=15\0110:00\0111:00\011-\012#\012# From Hernan G. Otero (1995-06-26):\012# From this moment on, the law that mandated the daylight saving\012# time corrections was derogated and no more modifications\012# to the time zones (for daylight saving) are now made.\012#\012# From Rives McDow (2000-01-10):\012# On October 3, 1999, 0:00 local, Argentina implemented daylight savings time,\012# which did not result in the switch of a time zone, as they stayed 9 hours\012# from the International Date Line.\012Rule\011Arg\0111999\011only\011-\011Oct\011Sun>=1\0110:00\0111:00\011-\012# From Paul Eggert (2007-12-28):\012# DST was set to expire on March 5, not March 3, but since it was converted\012# to standard time on March 3 it's more convenient for us to pretend that\012# it ended on March 3.\012Rule\011Arg\0112000\011only\011-\011Mar\0113\0110:00\0110\011-\012#\012");
result.append("# From Peter Gradelski via Steffen Thorsen (2000-03-01):\012# We just checked with our S\303\243o Paulo office and they say the government of\012# Argentina decided not to become one of the countries that go on or off DST.\012# So Buenos Aires should be -3 hours from GMT at all times.\012#\012# From Fabi\303\241n L. Arce Jofr\303\251 (2000-04-04):\012# The law that claimed DST for Argentina was derogated by President Fernando\012# de la R\303\272a on March 2, 2000, because it would make people spend more energy\012# in the winter time, rather than less.  The change took effect on March 3.\012#\012# From Mariano Absatz (2001-06-06):\012# one of the major newspapers here in Argentina said that the 1999\012# Timezone Law (which never was effectively applied) will (would?) be\012# in effect.... The article is at\012# http://ar.clarin.com/diario/2001-06-06/e-01701.htm\012# ... The Law itself is \042Ley No. 25155\042, sanctioned on 1999-08-25, enacted\012# 1999-09-17, and published 1999-09-21.  The official publication is at:\012# http://www.boletin.jus.gov.ar/BON/Primera/1999/09-Septiembre/21/PDF/BO21-09-99LEG.PDF\012# Regretfully, you have to subscribe (and pay) for the on-line version....\012#\012# (2001-06-12):\012# the timezone for Argentina will not change next Sunday.\012# Apparently it will do so on Sunday 24th....\012# http://ar.clarin.com/diario/2001-06-12/s-03501.htm\012#\012# (2001-06-25):\012# Last Friday (yes, the last working day before the date of the change), the\012# Senate annulled the 1999 law that introduced the changes later postponed.\012# http://www.clarin.com.ar/diario/2001-06-22/s-03601.htm\012# It remains the vote of the Deputies..., but it will be the same....\012# This kind of things had always been done this way in Argentina.\012# We are still -03:00 all year round in all of the country.\012#\012# From Steffen Thorsen (2007-12-21):\012# A user (Leonardo Chaim) reported that Argentina will adopt DST....\012# all of the country (all Zone-entries) are affected.  News reports like\012# http://www.lanacion.com.ar/opinion/nota.asp?nota_id=973037 indicate\012# that Argentina will use DST next year as well, from October to\012# March, although exact rules are not given.\012#\012# From Jesper N\303\270rgaard Welen (2007-12-26)\012# The last hurdle of Argentina DST is over, the proposal was approved in\012# the lower chamber too (Diputados) with a vote 192 for and 2 against.\012# By the way thanks to Mariano Absatz and Daniel Mario Vega for the link to\012# the original scanned proposal, where the dates and the zero hours are\012# clear and unambiguous...This is the article about final approval:\012# http://www.lanacion.com.ar/politica/nota.asp?nota_id=973996\012#\012# From Paul Eggert (2007-12-22):\012# For dates after mid-2008, the following rules are my guesses and\012# are quite possibly wrong, but are more likely than no DST at all.\012\012# From Alexander Krivenyshev (2008-09-05):\012# As per message from Carlos Alberto Fonseca Arauz (Nicaragua),\012# Argentina will start DST on Sunday October 19, 2008.\012#\012# http://www.worldtimezone.com/dst_news/dst_news_argentina03.html\012# http://www.impulsobaires.com.ar/nota.php?id=57832 (in spanish)\012\012# From Juan Manuel Docile in https://bugs.gentoo.org/240339 (2008-10-07)\012# via Rodrigo Severo:\012# Argentinian law No. 25.155 is no longer valid.\012# http://www.infoleg.gov.ar/infolegInternet/anexos/60000-64999/60036/norma.htm\012# The new one is law No. 26.350\012# http://www.infoleg.gov.ar/infolegInternet/anexos/135000-139999/136191/norma.htm\012# So there is no summer time in Argentina for now.\012\012# From Mariano Absatz (2008-10-20):\012# Decree 1693/2008 applies Law 26.350 for the summer 2008/2009 establishing DST\012# in Argentina from 2008-10-19 until 2009-03-15.\012# http://www.boletinoficial.gov.ar/Bora.Portal/CustomControls/PdfContent.aspx?fp=16102008&pi=3&pf=4&s=0&sec=01\012#\012\012# Decree 1705/2008 excepting 12 Provinces from applying DST in the summer\012# 2008/2009: Catamarca, La Rioja, Mendoza, Salta, San Juan, San Luis, La\012# Pampa, Neuqu\303\251n, Rio Negro, Chubut, Santa Cruz and Tierra del Fuego\012# http://www.boletinoficial.gov.ar/Bora.Portal/CustomControls/PdfContent.aspx?fp=17102008&pi=1&pf=1&");
result.append("s=0&sec=01\012#\012# Press release 235 dated Saturday October 18th, from the Government of the\012# Province of Jujuy saying it will not apply DST either (even when it was not\012# included in Decree 1705/2008).\012# http://www.jujuy.gov.ar/index2/partes_prensa/18_10_08/235-181008.doc\012\012# From fullinet (2009-10-18):\012# As announced in\012# http://www.argentina.gob.ar/argentina/portal/paginas.dhtml?pagina=356\012# (an official .gob.ar) under title: \042Sin Cambio de Hora\042\012# (English: \042No hour change\042).\012#\012# \042Por el momento, el Gobierno Nacional resolvi\303\263 no modificar la hora\012# oficial, decisi\303\263n que estaba en estudio para su implementaci\303\263n el\012# domingo 18 de octubre. Desde el Ministerio de Planificaci\303\263n se anunci\303\263\012# que la Argentina hoy, en estas condiciones meteorol\303\263gicas, no necesita\012# la modificaci\303\263n del huso horario, ya que 2009 nos encuentra con\012# crecimiento en la producci\303\263n y distribuci\303\263n energ\303\251tica.\042\012\012Rule\011Arg\0112007\011only\011-\011Dec\01130\0110:00\0111:00\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0112008\0112009\011-\011Mar\011Sun>=15\0110:00\0110\011-\012Rule\011Arg\0112008\011only\011-\011Oct\011Sun>=15\0110:00\0111:00\011-\012\012# From Mariano Absatz (2004-05-21):\012# Today it was officially published that the Province of Mendoza is changing\012# its timezone this winter... starting tomorrow night....\012# http://www.gobernac.mendoza.gov.ar/boletin/pdf/20040521-27158-normas.pdf\012# From Paul Eggert (2004-05-24):\012# It's Law No. 7,210.  This change is due to a public power emergency, so for\012# now we'll assume it's for this year only.\012#\012# From Paul Eggert (2018-01-31):\012# Hora de verano para la Rep\303\272blica Argentina\012# http://buenasiembra.com.ar/esoterismo/astrologia/hora-de-verano-de-la-republica-argentina-27.html\012# says that standard time in Argentina from 1894-10-31\012# to 1920-05-01 was -4:16:48.25.  Go with this more precise value\012# over Shanks & Pottenger.  It is upward compatible with Milne, who\012# says C\303\263rdoba time was -4:16:48.2.\012\012#\012# From Mariano Absatz (2004-06-05):\012# These media articles from a major newspaper mostly cover the current state:\012# http://www.lanacion.com.ar/04/05/27/de_604825.asp\012# http://www.lanacion.com.ar/04/05/28/de_605203.asp\012#\012# The following eight (8) provinces pulled clocks back to UTC-04:00 at\012# midnight Monday May 31st. (that is, the night between 05/31 and 06/01).\012# Apparently, all nine provinces would go back to UTC-03:00 at the same\012# time in October 17th.\012#\012# Catamarca, Chubut, La Rioja, San Juan, San Luis, Santa Cruz,\012# Tierra del Fuego, Tucum\303\241n.\012#\012# From Mariano Absatz (2004-06-14):\012# ... this weekend, the Province of Tucum\303\241n decided it'd go back to UTC-03:00\012# yesterday midnight (that is, at 24:00 Saturday 12th), since the people's\012# annoyance with the change is much higher than the power savings obtained....\012#\012# From Gwillim Law (2004-06-14):\012# http://www.lanacion.com.ar/04/06/10/de_609078.asp ...\012#     \042The time change in Tierra del Fuego was a conflicted decision from\012#   the start.  The government had decreed that the measure would take\012#   effect on June 1, but a normative error forced the new time to begin\012#   three days earlier, from a Saturday to a Sunday....\012# Our understanding was that the change was originally scheduled to take place\012# on June 1 at 00:00 in Chubut, Santa Cruz, Tierra del Fuego (and some other\012# provinces).  Sunday was May 30, only two days earlier.  So the article\012# contains a contradiction.  I would give more credence to the Saturday/Sunday\012# date than the \042three days earlier\042 phrase, and conclude that Tierra del\012# Fuego set its clocks back at 2004-05-30 00:00.\012#\012# From Steffen Thorsen (2004-10-05):\012# The previous law 7210 which changed the province of Mendoza's time zone\012# back in May have been modified slightly in a new law 7277, which set the\012# new end date to 2004-09-26 (original date was 2004-10-17).\012# http://www.gobernac.mendoza.gov.ar/boletin/pdf/20040924-27244-normas.pdf\012#\012# From Mariano Absatz (2004-10-05):\012# San Juan changed from UTC-03:00 to UTC-04:00 at midnight between\012# Sunday, May 30th and Monday, May 31st.  It changed back to UTC-03:00\012# at midnight between Saturday, Jul");
result.append("y 24th and Sunday, July 25th....\012# http://www.sanjuan.gov.ar/prensa/archivo/000329.html\012# http://www.sanjuan.gov.ar/prensa/archivo/000426.html\012# http://www.sanjuan.gov.ar/prensa/archivo/000441.html\012\012# From Alex Krivenyshev (2008-01-17):\012# Here are articles that Argentina Province San Luis is planning to end DST\012# as earlier as upcoming Monday January 21, 2008 or February 2008:\012#\012# Provincia argentina retrasa reloj y marca diferencia con resto del pa\303\255s\012# (Argentine Province delayed clock and mark difference with the rest of the\012# country)\012# http://cl.invertia.com/noticias/noticia.aspx?idNoticia=200801171849_EFE_ET4373&idtel\012#\012# Es inminente que en San Luis atrasen una hora los relojes\012# (It is imminent in San Luis clocks one hour delay)\012# https://www.lagaceta.com.ar/nota/253414/Economia/Es-inminente-que-en-San-Luis-atrasen-una-hora-los-relojes.html\012# http://www.worldtimezone.com/dst_news/dst_news_argentina02.html\012\012# From Jesper N\303\270rgaard Welen (2008-01-18):\012# The page of the San Luis provincial government\012# http://www.sanluis.gov.ar/notas.asp?idCanal=0&id=22812\012# confirms what Alex Krivenyshev has earlier sent to the tz\012# emailing list about that San Luis plans to return to standard\012# time much earlier than the rest of the country. It also\012# confirms that upon request the provinces San Juan and Mendoza\012# refused to follow San Luis in this change.\012#\012# The change is supposed to take place Monday the 21st at 0:00\012# hours. As far as I understand it if this goes ahead, we need\012# a new timezone for San Luis (although there are also documented\012# independent changes in the southamerica file of San Luis in\012# 1990 and 1991 which has not been confirmed).\012\012# From Jesper N\303\270rgaard Welen (2008-01-25):\012# Unfortunately the below page has become defunct, about the San Luis\012# time change. Perhaps because it now is part of a group of pages \042Most\012# important pages of 2008.\042\012#\012# You can use\012# http://www.sanluis.gov.ar/notas.asp?idCanal=8141&id=22834\012# instead it seems. Or use \042Buscador\042 from the main page of the San Luis\012# government, and fill in \042huso\042 and click OK, and you will get 3 pages\012# from which the first one is identical to the above.\012\012# From Mariano Absatz (2008-01-28):\012# I can confirm that the Province of San Luis (and so far only that\012# province) decided to go back to UTC-3 effective midnight Jan 20th 2008\012# (that is, Monday 21st at 0:00 is the time the clocks were delayed back\012# 1 hour), and they intend to keep UTC-3 as their timezone all year round\012# (that is, unless they change their mind any minute now).\012#\012# So we'll have to add yet another city to 'southamerica' (I think San\012# Luis city is the mos populated city in the Province, so it'd be\012# America/Argentina/San_Luis... of course I can't remember if San Luis's\012# history of particular changes goes along with Mendoza or San Juan :-(\012# (I only remember not being able to collect hard facts about San Luis\012# back in 2004, when these provinces changed to UTC-4 for a few days, I\012# mailed them personally and never got an answer).\012\012# From Paul Eggert (2014-08-12):\012# Unless otherwise specified, data entries are from Shanks & Pottenger through\012# 1992, from the IATA otherwise.  As noted below, Shanks & Pottenger say that\012# America/Cordoba split into 6 subregions during 1991/1992, one of which\012# was America/San_Luis, but we haven't verified this yet so for now we'll\012# keep America/Cordoba a single region rather than splitting it into the\012# other 5 subregions.\012\012# From Mariano Absatz (2009-03-13):\012# Yesterday (with our usual 2-day notice) the Province of San Luis\012# decided that next Sunday instead of \042staying\042 @utc-03:00 they will go\012# to utc-04:00 until the second Saturday in October...\012#\012# The press release is at\012# http://www.sanluis.gov.ar/SL/Paginas/NoticiaDetalle.asp?TemaId=1&InfoPrensaId=3102\012# (I couldn't find the decree, but www.sanluis.gov.ar\012# is the official page for the Province Government.)\012#\012# There's also a note in only one of the major national papers ...\012# http://www.lanacion.c");
result.append("om.ar/nota.asp?nota_id=1107912\012#\012# The press release says [quick and dirty translation]:\012# ... announced that next Sunday, at 00:00, Puntanos (the San Luis\012# inhabitants) will have to turn back one hour their clocks\012#\012# Since then, San Luis will establish its own Province timezone. Thus,\012# during 2009, this timezone change will run from 00:00 the third Sunday\012# in March until 24:00 of the second Saturday in October.\012\012# From Mariano Absatz (2009-10-16):\012# ...the Province of San Luis is a case in itself.\012#\012# The Law at\012# http://www.diputadossanluis.gov.ar/diputadosasp/paginas/verNorma.asp?NormaID=276\012# is ambiguous because establishes a calendar from the 2nd Sunday in\012# October at 0:00 thru the 2nd Saturday in March at 24:00 and the\012# complement of that starting on the 2nd Sunday of March at 0:00 and\012# ending on the 2nd Saturday of March at 24:00.\012#\012# This clearly breaks every time the 1st of March or October is a Sunday.\012#\012# IMHO, the \042spirit of the Law\042 is to make the changes at 0:00 on the 2nd\012# Sunday of October and March.\012#\012# The problem is that the changes in the rest of the Provinces that did\012# change in 2007/2008, were made according to the Federal Law and Decrees\012# that did so on the 3rd Sunday of October and March.\012#\012# In fact, San Luis actually switched from UTC-4 to UTC-3 last Sunday\012# (October 11th) at 0:00.\012#\012# So I guess a new set of rules, besides \042Arg\042, must be made and the last\012# America/Argentina/San_Luis entries should change to use these...\012# ...\012\012# From Alexander Krivenyshev (2010-04-09):\012# According to news reports from El Diario de la Rep\303\272blica Province San\012# Luis, Argentina (standard time UTC-04) will keep Daylight Saving Time\012# after April 11, 2010 - will continue to have same time as rest of\012# Argentina (UTC-3) (no DST).\012#\012# Confirmaron la pr\303\263rroga del huso horario de verano (Spanish)\012# http://www.eldiariodelarepublica.com/index.php?option=com_content&task=view&id=29383&Itemid=9\012# or (some English translation):\012# http://www.worldtimezone.com/dst_news/dst_news_argentina08.html\012\012# From Mariano Absatz (2010-04-12):\012# yes...I can confirm this...and given that San Luis keeps calling\012# UTC-03:00 \042summer time\042, we should't just let San Luis go back to \042Arg\042\012# rules...San Luis is still using \042Western ARgentina Time\042 and it got\012# stuck on Summer daylight savings time even though the summer is over.\012\012# From Paul Eggert (2018-01-23):\012# Perhaps San Luis operates on the legal fiction that it is at -04\012# with perpetual daylight saving time, but ordinary usage typically seems to\012# just say it's at -03; see, for example,\012# https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hora_oficial_argentina\012# We've documented similar situations as being plain changes to\012# standard time, so let's do that here too.  This does not change UTC\012# offsets, only tm_isdst and the time zone abbreviations.  One minor\012# plus is that this silences a zic complaint that there's no POSIX TZ\012# setting for timestamps past 2038.\012\012# Zone\011NAME\011\011STDOFF\011RULES\011FORMAT\011[UNTIL]\012#\012# Buenos Aires (BA), Capital Federal (CF),\012Zone America/Argentina/Buenos_Aires -3:53:48 - LMT\0111894 Oct 31\012\011\011#STDOFF\011-4:16:48.25\012\011\011\011-4:16:48 -\011CMT\0111920 May    # C\303\263rdoba Mean Time\012\011\011\011-4:00\011-\011%z\0111930 Dec\012\011\011\011-4:00\011Arg\011%z\0111969 Oct  5\012\011\011\011-3:00\011Arg\011%z\0111999 Oct  3\012\011\011\011-4:00\011Arg\011%z\0112000 Mar  3\012\011\011\011-3:00\011Arg\011%z\012#\012# C\303\263rdoba (CB), Santa Fe (SF), Entre R\303\255os (ER), Corrientes (CN), Misiones (MN),\012# Chaco (CC), Formosa (FM), Santiago del Estero (SE)\012#\012# Shanks & Pottenger also make the following claims, which we haven't verified:\012# - Formosa switched to -3:00 on 1991-01-07.\012# - Misiones switched to -3:00 on 1990-12-29.\012# - Chaco switched to -3:00 on 1991-01-04.\012# - Santiago del Estero switched to -4:00 on 1991-04-01,\012#   then to -3:00 on 1991-04-26.\012#\012\011\011#STDOFF\011       -4:16:48.25\012Zone America/Argentina/Cordoba -4:16:48 - LMT\0111894 Oct 31\012\011\011\011-4:16:48 -\011CMT\0111920 May\012\011\011\011-4:00\011-\011%z\0111930 Dec\012\011\011\011-4:00\011Arg\011%z\0111969 Oct  5\012\011\011\011-3:00\011Arg\011%z\0111991 Mar  3\012\011\011\011-4:00\011-\011%z\0111991 Oct 20\012\011\011\011-3:00\011Arg\011%z\0111999 Oct  3\012\011\011\011-4:00\011Arg\011%z\0112000");
